{{Short description|None}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=June 2018}}
Group C of the [[2010 FIFA World Cup]] began on 12 June and ended on 23 June 2010. The group consisted of national [[association football]] teams from [[England national football team|England]], the [[United States men's national soccer team|United States]], [[Algeria national football team|Algeria]] and [[Slovenia national football team|Slovenia]]. The United States and England progressed to the round of 16 undefeated. The United States won the group, winning one match and drawing two. England had an identical record and goal difference but the United States finished top having scored more goals than England. Slovenia finished the group third having won a game, with Algeria finishing fourth with a single point.

Only England and the United States had previously met at a World Cup in [[1950 FIFA World Cup|1950]], when the [[United States v England (1950 FIFA World Cup)|United States defeated England 1–0]]. 

==Teams==
The teams were decided by [[2010 FIFA World Cup seeding|World Cup draw]] that took place on 4 December 2009.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/internationals/8393328.stm |title=England face USA, Algeria & Slovenia in 2010 World Cup |publisher=BBC Sport |date=4 December 2009 |access-date=27 July 2023}}</ref> The group was set to receive one team from each pot, which sorted all World Cup teams by position on the [[FIFA World Rankings]] as of October 2009.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2009/dec/02/world-cup-2010-draw-details |title=World Cup 2010 draw: the details |publisher=The Guardian |date=2 December 2009 |access-date=27 July 2023}}</ref> 

The first team drawn was England, which secured qualification as a pot one team by winning [[2010 FIFA World Cup qualification – UEFA Group 6|Group 6 of UEFA qualification]].<ref name="UEFA">{{cite news|url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2010q.html#eur |title=World Cup 2010 Qualifying#UEFA |publisher=RSSSF |access-date=28 July 2023}}</ref> The United States finished top of the [[2010 FIFA World Cup qualification – CONCACAF fourth round|CONCACAF fourth round]] group.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2010q.html#nca |title=World Cup 2010 qualifying#North and Central America |publisher=RSSSF |access-date=28 July 2023}}</ref> The pot 3 team was Algeria who qualified for the World Cup for the first time since [[1986 FIFA World Cup|1986]] thanks to a one-off play-off win against [[Egypt national football team|Egypt]].<ref name="ALGERIA86">{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/africa/8367431.stm |title=Yahia sends Algeria to World Cup |publisher=BBC Sport |date=18 November 2009 |access-date=28 July 2023}}</ref> The play-off tie was played in [[Khartoum]] after the two sides finished level with each other in Group C.<ref name="ALGERIA86"/> Post-match, a diplomatic spat occurred between the two nations with Egypt later threatening to quit international football for two years due to the alleged behaviour of Algerian fans.<ref name="EGYPT">{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/8369983.stm |title=Egypt-Algeria World Cup anger turns violent in Cairo |publisher=BBC News |date=20 November 2009 |access-date=28 July 2023}}</ref> There were also violent demonstrations in [[Cairo]] near the Algerian embassy.<ref name="EGYPT"/> The final team was Slovenia who finished second in [[2010 FIFA World Cup qualification – UEFA Group 3|Group 3 of UEFA qualification]], two points behind [[Slovakia national football team|Slovakia]].<ref name="UEFA"/> In the subsequent play-offs, Slovenia defeated [[Russia national football team|Russia]] in a two-legged tie to qualify for the finals.<ref name="UEFA"/><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2009/nov/18/slovenia-russia-greece-ukraine-world-cup |title=Dedic squeezes Slovenia through as nine-man Russia implode |author=Gregg Roughley |publisher=The Guardian |date=18 November 2009 |access-date=28 July 2023}}</ref>

England were considered favourites to win Group C.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2010/jun/04/world-cup-2010-england-fabio-capello |title=World Cup 2010: England's eyes fixed on the greatest prize |author=Richard Williams |publisher=The Guardian |date=4 June 2010 |access-date=28 July 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/401877-2010-world-cup-group-c-preview-england-usa-algeria-slovenia |title=2010 World Cup Group C Preview: England, USA, Algeria, Slovenia |publisher=Bleacher Report |date=5 June 2010 |access-date=29 July 2023}}</ref> In a pre-tournament preview, [[The Guardian]]'s [[Kevin McCarra]] analysed England's chances ahead of the tournament: "If [[Wayne Rooney]] stayed fit, if [[Steven Gerrard]] got his form back and if the centre-backs were in rude health, England would present a severe problem to most. [[Fabio Capello|Capello]] needs to refresh a dynamism that has waned. Semi-finalists."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jun/05/england-world-cup-2010-team-guide |title=England: World Cup 2010 team guide |author=Kevin McCarra |publisher=The Guardian |date=5 June 2010 |access-date=28 July 2023}}</ref> The Guardian previewed the US' chances: "The USA have been quietly optimistic about their chances of making a splash in South Africa ever since reaching the Confederations Cup final last year, where they lost to Brazil. They are confident of making it out of what is perceived to be a weak group and believe they can challenge England for first place."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/may/26/world-cup-brazil-spain-italy-argentina |title=World Cup 2010: A guide to favourites, nearly men and party spoilers |publisher=The Guardian |date=26 May 2010 |access-date=29 July 2023}}</ref>

